{
 "id": "t_feedback",
 "group": "thread",
 "num": 3,
 "title": "Feedback Loops",
 "subtitle": "Homeostasis in temperature, calcium, sugar, oxygen and birth",
 "lectures": "Lectures 1, 6, 7, 14, 15",
 "voice": "af_heart",
 "scenes": [
  {
   "lec": "L1 · Homeostasis",
   "title": "The four parts of a feedback loop",
   "img": "L1_13.jpg",
   "terms": [
    "Homeostasis",
    "Stimulus",
    "Receptor",
    "Control center",
    "Effector"
   ],
   "start": 0.4,
   "tstart": 0.4,
   "tend": 2.15,
   "lines": [
    {
     "text": "**Homeostasis** keeps the inside of the body stable.",
     "start": 2.85,
     "end": 6.13
    },
    {
     "text": "Every feedback loop has four parts.",
     "start": 6.58,
     "end": 8.52
    },
    {
     "text": "A **stimulus**, a **receptor**, a **control center** and an **effector**.",
     "start": 8.97,
     "end": 12.5
    },
    {
     "text": "Once you see this pattern, you'll find it all through the midterm.",
     "start": 12.95,
     "end": 16.16
    }
   ],
   "end": 17.26
  },
  {
   "lec": "L1 · Feedback",
   "title": "Negative and positive feedback",
   "img": null,
   "terms": [
    "Negative",
    "Positive"
   ],
   "start": 17.26,
   "tstart": 17.26,
   "tend": 19.27,
   "lines": [
    {
     "text": "In **negative** feedback, the response opposes the change. It restores normal.",
     "start": 19.97,
     "end": 24.61
    },
    {
     "text": "Most feedback is negative.",
     "start": 25.06,
     "end": 26.62
    },
    {
     "text": "In **positive** feedback, the response amplifies the change.",
     "start": 27.07,
     "end": 30.63
    },
    {
     "text": "It's rare. Childbirth and blood clotting.",
     "start": 31.08,
     "end": 33.36
    }
   ],
   "end": 34.46
  },
  {
   "lec": "L1 + L6 · Temperature",
   "title": "Temperature: too cold",
   "img": "L1_14.jpg",
   "terms": [
    "Krause",
    "Hypothalamus",
    "Vasoconstriction",
    "Shivering"
   ],
   "start": 34.46,
   "tstart": 34.46,
   "tend": 35.96,
   "lines": [
    {
     "text": "Stimulus: an ice cube cools the skin.",
     "start": 36.66,
     "end": 39.02
    },
    {
     "text": "Receptor: a **Krause** bulb in the skin senses cold.",
     "start": 39.47,
     "end": 42.47
    },
    {
     "text": "Control center: the **hypothalamus**.",
     "start": 42.92,
     "end": 45.24
    },
    {
     "text": "Effectors: vessels constrict, and skeletal muscles **shiver**.",
     "start": 45.69,
     "end": 49.01
    },
    {
     "text": "Temperature rises back toward 98.6. That's negative feedback.",
     "start": 49.46,
     "end": 54.12
    }
   ],
   "end": 55.22
  },
  {
   "lec": "L6 · Temperature",
   "title": "Temperature: too hot",
   "img": null,
   "terms": [
    "Sweat",
    "Vasodilation",
    "Eccrine glands"
   ],
   "start": 55.22,
   "tstart": 55.22,
   "tend": 56.64,
   "lines": [
    {
     "text": "When you're too warm, the response flips.",
     "start": 57.34,
     "end": 59.4
    },
    {
     "text": "**Eccrine glands** release **sweat**, and it evaporates.",
     "start": 59.85,
     "end": 62.58
    },
    {
     "text": "Skin vessels dilate to shed heat. That's **vasodilation**.",
     "start": 63.03,
     "end": 66.57
    },
    {
     "text": "The skin chapter called this thermoregulation. It's the same loop.",
     "start": 67.02,
     "end": 70.9
    }
   ],
   "end": 72.0
  },
  {
   "lec": "L7 + L15 · Calcium",
   "title": "Blood calcium too high",
   "img": "L7_17.jpg",
   "terms": [
    "Thyroid",
    "Calcitonin",
    "Osteoblasts"
   ],
   "start": 72.0,
   "tstart": 72.0,
   "tend": 73.38,
   "lines": [
    {
     "text": "Stimulus: blood calcium rises.",
     "start": 74.08,
     "end": 76.32
    },
    {
     "text": "Receptor and control center: the **thyroid** gland.",
     "start": 76.77,
     "end": 79.66
    },
    {
     "text": "It releases **calcitonin**.",
     "start": 80.11,
     "end": 81.62
    },
    {
     "text": "Effector: bone. **Osteoblasts** pull calcium into bone.",
     "start": 82.07,
     "end": 85.63
    },
    {
     "text": "Blood calcium falls back to normal.",
     "start": 86.08,
     "end": 88.05
    }
   ],
   "end": 89.15
  },
  {
   "lec": "L15 · Calcium",
   "title": "Blood calcium too low",
   "img": "L15_04.jpg",
   "terms": [
    "Parathyroids",
    "PTH",
    "Osteoclasts",
    "Humoral"
   ],
   "start": 89.15,
   "tstart": 89.15,
   "tend": 90.46,
   "lines": [
    {
     "text": "Stimulus: blood calcium drops.",
     "start": 91.16,
     "end": 93.26
    },
    {
     "text": "The **parathyroids** sense it directly. That's a **humoral** stimulus.",
     "start": 93.71,
     "end": 97.3
    },
    {
     "text": "They release **PTH**.",
     "start": 97.75,
     "end": 99.48
    },
    {
     "text": "**Osteoclasts** release calcium from bone into blood.",
     "start": 99.93,
     "end": 102.79
    },
    {
     "text": "Two hormones, pulling opposite ways. That's how a set point holds.",
     "start": 103.24,
     "end": 107.14
    }
   ],
   "end": 108.24
  },
  {
   "lec": "L1 + L15 · Sugar",
   "title": "Blood sugar too high",
   "img": null,
   "terms": [
    "Beta cells",
    "Insulin",
    "Glycogenesis"
   ],
   "start": 108.24,
   "tstart": 108.24,
   "tend": 109.45,
   "lines": [
    {
     "text": "Stimulus: you eat cake, and blood sugar rises.",
     "start": 110.15,
     "end": 113.09
    },
    {
     "text": "Receptors on the pancreas sense it.",
     "start": 113.54,
     "end": 115.38
    },
    {
     "text": "**Beta cells** in the islets release **insulin**.",
     "start": 115.83,
     "end": 118.37
    },
    {
     "text": "Cells take in glucose, and the liver stores it as glycogen. That's **glycogenesis**.",
     "start": 118.82,
     "end": 124.17
    },
    {
     "text": "Blood sugar falls.",
     "start": 124.62,
     "end": 125.75
    }
   ],
   "end": 126.85
  },
  {
   "lec": "L1 + L15 · Sugar",
   "title": "Blood sugar too low",
   "img": null,
   "terms": [
    "Alpha cells",
    "Glucagon",
    "Glycogenolysis"
   ],
   "start": 126.85,
   "tstart": 126.85,
   "tend": 128.05,
   "lines": [
    {
     "text": "Stimulus: you haven't eaten, and blood sugar drops.",
     "start": 128.75,
     "end": 131.77
    },
    {
     "text": "**Alpha cells** release **glucagon**.",
     "start": 132.22,
     "end": 133.93
    },
    {
     "text": "The liver breaks glycogen down. That's **glycogenolysis**.",
     "start": 134.38,
     "end": 138.04
    },
    {
     "text": "Blood sugar rises.",
     "start": 138.49,
     "end": 139.74
    },
    {
     "text": "In type 1 diabetes, there's no insulin. In type 2, cells don't respond to it.",
     "start": 140.19,
     "end": 145.43
    }
   ],
   "end": 146.53
  },
  {
   "lec": "L15 · Oxygen",
   "title": "Blood oxygen too low",
   "img": null,
   "terms": [
    "Kidneys",
    "EPO",
    "Red marrow"
   ],
   "start": 146.53,
   "tstart": 146.53,
   "tend": 147.9,
   "lines": [
    {
     "text": "Stimulus: blood oxygen drops, from bleeding or high altitude.",
     "start": 148.6,
     "end": 152.65
    },
    {
     "text": "Receptors in the **kidneys** sense it.",
     "start": 153.1,
     "end": 154.89
    },
    {
     "text": "They release **EPO**, erythropoietin.",
     "start": 155.34,
     "end": 157.09
    },
    {
     "text": "Effector: **red marrow** makes more red blood cells.",
     "start": 157.54,
     "end": 160.3
    },
    {
     "text": "More cells carry more oxygen. The loop closes.",
     "start": 160.75,
     "end": 163.85
    }
   ],
   "end": 164.95
  },
  {
   "lec": "L15 · Blood pressure",
   "title": "Blood pressure and water balance",
   "img": null,
   "terms": [
    "Renin",
    "Aldosterone",
    "ADH"
   ],
   "start": 164.95,
   "tstart": 164.95,
   "tend": 166.72,
   "lines": [
    {
     "text": "When blood pressure or sodium drops, the kidneys release **renin**.",
     "start": 167.42,
     "end": 171.15
    },
    {
     "text": "That system ends with **aldosterone**, which holds sodium.",
     "start": 171.6,
     "end": 174.82
    },
    {
     "text": "**ADH** from the posterior pituitary holds water.",
     "start": 175.27,
     "end": 179.28
    },
    {
     "text": "Both raise blood volume and pressure.",
     "start": 179.73,
     "end": 181.7
    }
   ],
   "end": 182.8
  },
  {
   "lec": "L1 + L15 · Birth",
   "title": "Positive feedback: childbirth",
   "img": "L1_15.jpg",
   "terms": [
    "Cervix",
    "Oxytocin",
    "Contractions"
   ],
   "start": 182.8,
   "tstart": 182.8,
   "tend": 184.86,
   "lines": [
    {
     "text": "Stimulus: the baby stretches the **cervix**.",
     "start": 185.56,
     "end": 188.02
    },
    {
     "text": "Receptors in the uterus signal the hypothalamus.",
     "start": 188.47,
     "end": 191.63
    },
    {
     "text": "The posterior pituitary releases **oxytocin**.",
     "start": 192.08,
     "end": 194.94
    },
    {
     "text": "**Contractions** get stronger, which stretches the cervix more.",
     "start": 195.39,
     "end": 198.64
    },
    {
     "text": "The loop amplifies until the baby is born. Then it stops.",
     "start": 199.09,
     "end": 202.64
    }
   ],
   "end": 203.74
  },
  {
   "lec": "L14 · Muscle",
   "title": "Muscle as the effector",
   "img": null,
   "terms": [
    "Effector",
    "Motor output",
    "Smooth muscle"
   ],
   "start": 203.74,
   "tstart": 203.74,
   "tend": 205.14,
   "lines": [
    {
     "text": "Notice how often the **effector** is muscle.",
     "start": 205.84,
     "end": 208.16
    },
    {
     "text": "Skeletal muscle shivers. Smooth muscle in vessel walls constricts or dilates.",
     "start": 208.61,
     "end": 213.58
    },
    {
     "text": "The uterus contracts in birth.",
     "start": 214.03,
     "end": 215.64
    },
    {
     "text": "That's the nervous system's **motor output**, from Lecture 14.",
     "start": 216.09,
     "end": 219.63
    }
   ],
   "end": 220.73
  },
  {
   "lec": "Recap",
   "title": "Recap: spotting a feedback loop",
   "img": null,
   "terms": [
    "Temperature",
    "Calcium",
    "Glucose",
    "Oxygen",
    "Birth"
   ],
   "start": 220.73,
   "tstart": 220.73,
   "tend": 222.53,
   "lines": [
    {
     "text": "On the exam, look for the four parts.",
     "start": 223.23,
     "end": 225.33
    },
    {
     "text": "What changed? What sensed it? What decided? What responded?",
     "start": 225.78,
     "end": 229.31
    },
    {
     "text": "Then ask: did the response oppose the change, or amplify it?",
     "start": 229.76,
     "end": 233.49
    },
    {
     "text": "**Temperature**, **calcium**, **glucose** and **oxygen** are negative.",
     "start": 233.94,
     "end": 237.62
    },
    {
     "text": "**Birth** and clotting are positive.",
     "start": 238.07,
     "end": 239.9
    }
   ],
   "end": 241.0
  }
 ],
 "duration": 241.0,
 "audio": "stories/t_feedback.mp3"
}